Count Subsequences with Specific Property (Sum == Target)

Problem Statement:
Find the total number of subsequences of an array that sum up to a specific target integer.

Input: arr = [1, 2, 1] target = 2
Output: 2
The subsequences are [1, 1] at indices 0 and 2 and 
[2] at index 1. 
So total count = 2

int countSubsequences(int ind, int currentSum, vector<int>& arr, int target, int n) {
    //Base condition
    if (ind == n) {
        if (currentSum == target) return 1; //Found 1 valid subsequence
        else return 0; //Not valid 
    }

    //Take the element 
    int take = countSubsequences(ind + 1, currentSum + arr[ind], arr, target, n);

    //Not take the element 
    int notTake = countSubsequences(ind + 1, currentSum, arr, target, n);

    //Return total count from both branches 
    return take + nonTake;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> arr = {1, 2, 1};
    int target = 2;
    cout << "Count: " << countSubsequences(0, 0, arr, target, arr.size()) << endl;
    return 0;
}

TC: O(2^n)
We explore two choices (take or not take) for every element.
(Can be optimized to O(n * target) using DP/Memoization)

SC: O(n)
For recursion call stack space.

